<header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">HUMAN GOLD RUSH LESSON</p><h1>Technology & Research</h1><p class="intro">The proposed research system preserves study-relevant behavioral events while minimizing human input and avoiding heavy personal-data imports.</p><blockquote>How can technology preserve a reproducible dataset without building a surveillance system?</blockquote></header><aside class="notice"><strong>Important distinction</strong><p>Human-subjects classification, consent language, retention, withdrawal handling, and data release require review by the responsible IRB or authorized institutional officials.</p></aside><section class="content-grid"><article><span>01</span><div><h2>Minimum human input</h2><p>Consent; participate, observe, or walk away; accept or reject; final destination yes or no; and exception actions such as dispute, correct, or withdraw.</p></div></article><article><span>02</span><div><h2>Automatic context</h2><p>The system supplies quarter, event, pseudonymous device, QR route, timestamp, approved location or proximity, role-at-event, state transition, consent version, schema version, and a consensus-anchored hash of the accepted event.</p></div></article><article><span>03</span><div><h2>No heavy imports</h2><p>Exclude church rosters, merchant customer files, contact uploads, POS histories, social scraping, message-content analysis, banking records, voter files, and continuous location history.</p></div></article><article><span>04</span><div><h2>Research-ready preservation</h2><p>Maintain an append-only source log, protected identity-linkage vault, de-identified research dataset, public aggregate dataset, codebook, consent history, transformation record, and checksums.</p></div></article><article><span>05</span><div><h2>Proposal status</h2><p>AYSPS and Santa Fe Institute are prospective research audiences or collaborators. Publication must not imply institutional acceptance, sponsorship, IRB approval, or partnership until formally documented.</p></div></article><article><span>06</span><div><h2>Pre-genesis block</h2><p>The next few years are a pattern-testing period: every capture happens inside a 50-meter, 3-minute buyer/recipient and seller/sender handshake, confirmed through the <a href="proof-of-fulfillment.html">three universal QR codes</a> — Identity, Banking/YAM-is-ON, and Thanking/Seeking Gratitude — before any wider rollout is considered.</p></div></article><article><span>07</span><div><h2>Four responses, one primary key</h2><p>Participate (MEGAvoter), Observe (YAM’er), Walk Away, and Unreached are quantified as behavioral research categories, not identities. UUID recognition — a pseudonymous device ID — is the primary key tying a response to an event; it is never a name, account, or financial identifier.</p></div></article><article><span>08</span><div><h2>Four access points, one leaderboard</h2><p>A YAM-is-ON voucher, a Seeking Gratitude hang tag or sticker, an RSVP postcard, and a Peace Pentagon business card are four physical access points. YAM-is-ON connects reported $30 commerce to Presence evidence; Seeking Gratitude records nonfinancial service. Public dashboards keep revenue and XP visibly separate.</p></div></article><article><span>09</span><div><h2>$0, 0%, twelve weeks</h2><p>The $0 WooCommerce backorder records intent and reserves fulfillment; it carries no interest or fee. The study asks whether RSVP intent becomes confirmed action, and whether YAM-is-ON commerce or Seeking Gratitude service builds momentum faster across the twelve-week quarter.</p></div></article><article><span>10</span><div><h2>Decoupling storage from trust</h2><p>Raw scan data stays off-chain; only a hash of an accepted, throttled event is anchored to a Hedera Consensus Service (HCS) topic, which timestamps it in a fair, tamper-evident order no single server can rewrite. A 50-meter, 3-minute throttle — checked by real distance, not just a grid cell — rejects implausible claims before they ever reach the ledger, and nearby devices can corroborate a claim through decayed (not linear) witness weighting.</p></div></article><article><span>11</span><div><h2>Independently checkable</h2><p>XP is never a stored balance — it's computed by replaying scan events, with a periodic Merkle-root checkpoint anchored to HCS. Anyone — auditor, regulator, or participant — can independently verify a scan or checkpoint through the public Hedera Mirror Node, without trusting Codepixelzmedia's servers.</p></div></article><article><span>12</span><div><h2>Stored value would be a false signal</h2><p>A stored balance can be edited, inflated, or spent without anyone showing up — which is exactly why this system refuses to keep one. What proves participation is the trading history of XP itself, verified against a registered device credential (a smartphone or other UUID-bearing device) at the moment of each scan. Credential and event, not a saved number, are the proof.</p></div></article><article><span>13</span><div><h2>Testnet to mainnet, on two dates</h2><p>May 17, 2030 is the target genesis moment — the point where this pilot's testnet activity gives way to a mainnet foundation, with every prior HCS-anchored hash standing as the proof trail that carries over. September 1, 2030 is the first mainnet settlement, the same annual Redemption Day rhythm already in place, just on a larger foundation.</p></div></article><article><span>14</span><div><h2>A 1% control group, kept for ten years</h2><p>Mainnet does not close the testnet down. A deliberate 1% of activity remains in the testnet environment for the ten years following genesis — a living control group that keeps presence measurable in its original, pre-mainnet form, so the mainnet dataset always has something to be compared against.</p></div></article><article><span>15</span><div><h2>A ledger shaped like attention</h2><p>Graphed across a quarter, a sustained XP ledger doesn’t climb in a straight line — it moves in short bursts and pauses, mathematically closer to how an eye saccades across a scene than to a steady meter running up. That’s a description of the shape the data already takes, not a new capture: no camera, no gaze-tracking hardware, nothing added to the four access points above. It’s simply the pattern presence leaves behind when it’s genuinely intermittent, not constant.</p></div></article><article><span>16</span><div><h2>Gratitude, captured by presence, not by gaze</h2><p>The same shape shows up in Thanking/Seeking Gratitude QR data specifically: recognition clusters around real, discrete moments of showing up rather than arriving as a continuous stream. That clustering is what the eye-movement metaphor points at — it is a description, not a proposal to read anyone’s eyes. The Thanking scan itself, not any biometric signal, remains the only thing that records gratitude.</p></div></article></section><section><div class="section-heading"><p>EVENT-DRIVEN RESEARCH</p><h2>One scan, one choice, one append-only event</h2></div><div class="flow"><div>Device registration</div><b>→</b><div>Consent</div><b>→</b><div>QR interaction</div><b>→</b><div>Throttle check</div><b>→</b><div>Human choice</div><b>→</b><div>HCS anchor</div><b>→</b><div>Quarter dataset</div></div></section><section class="content-grid"><article><span>17</span><div><h2>Seller as VFN</h2><p>A MEGAvoter seller posts a month-end testament of YAM-is-ON fiat activity directly to the XP append-only General Ledger. The posting is the seller’s declaration, not proof that the ledger received or held money.</p></div></article><article><span>18</span><div><h2>Quarterly integrity</h2><p>Three monthly seller testaments, two-device events, RSVP intent, fulfillment outcomes, and unresolved disputes close as one quarterly behavioral dataset. Eligible monetary obligations settle outside the XP ledger; Presence remains append-only evidence.</p></div></article></section>
